WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:03.929
This is the technical deep
dive for Book 11 of the

3
00:00:03.929 --> 00:00:07.887
Federal Modernization Workbook,
Zero Trust and Assessment.

4
00:00:07.887 --> 00:00:10.942
It's for the people who
actually run the assessment:

5
00:00:10.942 --> 00:00:13.050
security engineers and assessors.

6
00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:15.025
The whole book turns on one idea.

7
00:00:15.025 --> 00:00:19.736
Zero Trust belongs to the whole agency,
not to a product or a tenant score,

8
00:00:19.736 --> 00:00:23.488
and the assessment tools hand
you evidence, not a verdict.

9
00:00:23.488 --> 00:00:28.150
Part 1 covers what the mandates really
require and what the tools really measure.

10
00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:30.515
Part 2 covers who owns the decisions,

11
00:00:30.515 --> 00:00:33.825
and how to run the assessment
so the output holds up.

12
00:00:33.825 --> 00:00:36.010
Let's start with why this matters.

13
00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:41.460
Two incidents make the case.

14
00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:46.226
In 2023, tokens forged with a provider's
own stolen signing key read federal

15
00:00:46.226 --> 00:00:51.222
email for weeks, and every system that
checked those tokens said they were fine.

16
00:00:51.222 --> 00:00:54.747
What caught it was one department's
own logging, not the provider.

17
00:00:54.747 --> 00:00:56.847
Then there's the layer beneath the guest.

18
00:00:56.847 --> 00:01:01.323
Ransomware crews went after hypervisors
directly, and one flaw handed full

19
00:01:01.323 --> 00:01:05.585
control to anyone who could create a
directory group with the right name.

20
00:01:05.585 --> 00:01:09.322
The security tools inside each
virtual machine never saw it.

21
00:01:09.322 --> 00:01:13.298
In both cases, the failure sat
where no single tool was looking.

22
00:01:13.298 --> 00:01:15.650
That's what Module 1 builds from.

23
00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:21.313
Module 1 sets the target.

24
00:01:21.313 --> 00:01:24.942
We'll look at what the federal
guidance actually says about scope,

25
00:01:24.942 --> 00:01:27.967
why governance is the
capability programs underfund,

26
00:01:27.967 --> 00:01:30.925
and which documents are
binding and which are guides.

27
00:01:30.925 --> 00:01:34.580
Getting that straight is how you
scope an assessment honestly.

28
00:01:38.330 --> 00:01:39.717
Here's the core argument.

29
00:01:39.717 --> 00:01:44.025
Every federal Zero Trust document
describes the enterprise:

30
00:01:44.025 --> 00:01:49.093
all identities, all devices, every
network, cloud and virtualized estate.

31
00:01:49.093 --> 00:01:51.217
None of them is scoped to a product.

32
00:01:51.217 --> 00:01:56.188
But most agencies assess the network, the
clouds and the hypervisors separately,

33
00:01:56.188 --> 00:02:00.193
each with its own inventory, and
then staple the reports together.

34
00:02:00.193 --> 00:02:04.261
The same server shows up as three
different objects to three assessors,

35
00:02:04.261 --> 00:02:06.881
so the total doesn't
measure one architecture.

36
00:02:06.881 --> 00:02:09.418
A scanner can confirm
that things are unified.

37
00:02:09.418 --> 00:02:10.705
It can't unify them.

38
00:02:10.705 --> 00:02:15.520
Next, why programs stall even after
buying a tool for every pillar.

39
00:02:19.270 --> 00:02:23.658
The maturity model looks like five
pillars, so programs buy five tools.

40
00:02:23.658 --> 00:02:27.968
But underneath sit three capabilities
that run the pillars together,

41
00:02:27.968 --> 00:02:30.346
and governance is the one nobody sells.

42
00:02:30.346 --> 00:02:31.945
That's why programs stall.

43
00:02:31.945 --> 00:02:36.590
Pillars get administered but not
governed, evidence lives in each tool's

44
00:02:36.590 --> 00:02:41.158
format and can't be re-run, and drift
turns up only when an auditor finds it.

45
00:02:41.158 --> 00:02:46.058
The model's own text says advanced maturity
means coordination across pillars.

46
00:02:46.058 --> 00:02:48.807
Another per-pillar tool
doesn't move that needle.

47
00:02:48.807 --> 00:02:49.732
So what does?

48
00:02:49.732 --> 00:02:54.060
Five governance principles, starting
with identity and privilege.

49
00:02:57.810 --> 00:02:59.110
First, identity.

50
00:02:59.110 --> 00:03:03.542
The program makes identity the root
that everything else is named through,

51
00:03:03.542 --> 00:03:05.972
but that's a naming
claim, not a trust claim.

52
00:03:05.972 --> 00:03:08.967
The 2023 forged tokens passed every check,

53
00:03:08.967 --> 00:03:13.073
so the identity plane is the
highest-value target you have.

54
00:03:13.073 --> 00:03:15.657
That means sessions
you can revoke quickly,

55
00:03:15.657 --> 00:03:19.247
phishing-resistant sign-in, and
its own monitored log stream.

56
00:03:19.247 --> 00:03:20.798
Second, privilege.

57
00:03:20.798 --> 00:03:24.248
Standing admin access is
the model's lowest stage.

58
00:03:24.248 --> 00:03:28.682
Make it eligible and time-boxed, keep
emergency accounts tested and watched,

59
00:03:28.682 --> 00:03:32.623
and never grant admin rights just
because a group has a certain name.

60
00:03:32.623 --> 00:03:37.400
Next, the principles about evidence,
enforcement and the boundary.

61
00:03:41.150 --> 00:03:44.099
Principle three: evidence
has to be something an

62
00:03:44.099 --> 00:03:46.975
assessor can re-run and
get the same answer from.

63
00:03:46.975 --> 00:03:49.800
If it can't be reproduced,
it's an assertion.

64
00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:55.425
Principle four: a policy that only produces
a dashboard is a report, not a control.

65
00:03:55.425 --> 00:03:58.072
Enforcement can still wait
for a human approval,

66
00:03:58.072 --> 00:04:01.712
but something has to happen when
the running state breaks the rule.

67
00:04:01.712 --> 00:04:04.450
Principle five: model the cloud boundary.

68
00:04:04.450 --> 00:04:07.636
Government clouds don't have
every commercial feature,

69
00:04:07.636 --> 00:04:09.575
and each mandate stops somewhere.

70
00:04:09.575 --> 00:04:12.150
Write those gaps down
as governed findings.

71
00:04:12.150 --> 00:04:15.500
Now, which documents
actually set requirements?

72
00:04:19.250 --> 00:04:22.159
Zero Trust programs
get argued by citation,

73
00:04:22.159 --> 00:04:24.825
and most citations don't survive a check.

74
00:04:24.825 --> 00:04:28.725
Only the executive order and
the OMB memo are binding.

75
00:04:28.725 --> 00:04:32.148
CISA's maturity model calls
itself a general guide,

76
00:04:32.148 --> 00:04:34.775
and NIST's architecture is guidance too.

77
00:04:34.775 --> 00:04:37.656
Other instruments bind
particular surfaces,

78
00:04:37.656 --> 00:04:40.538
like cloud tenant
configuration and logging.

79
00:04:40.538 --> 00:04:43.450
And several popular claims
simply don't exist.

80
00:04:43.450 --> 00:04:47.786
There's no required maturity stage,
no federal Zero Trust score,

81
00:04:47.786 --> 00:04:50.538
and no rule that you decrypt all traffic.

82
00:04:50.538 --> 00:04:53.225
Knowing that saves a
lot of misdirected work.

83
00:04:53.225 --> 00:04:56.390
So what does the OMB
memo actually require?

84
00:05:00.140 --> 00:05:03.393
The OMB memo sets specific
actions for each pillar,

85
00:05:03.393 --> 00:05:06.103
and they're more precise
than people quote.

86
00:05:06.103 --> 00:05:10.988
Phishing-resistant sign-in has a definition,
and it means turning off text codes,

87
00:05:10.988 --> 00:05:14.615
one-time codes and push approvals
for routine staff access.

88
00:05:14.615 --> 00:05:18.140
The network action is an
isolation plan, not a product.

89
00:05:18.140 --> 00:05:20.915
One device signal is
the floor, not the goal.

90
00:05:20.915 --> 00:05:25.759
The deadlines have passed, but nothing
rescinded the memo, so treat its actions as

91
00:05:25.759 --> 00:05:30.603
an existing obligation, and read its
logging line through the newer logging memo.

92
00:05:30.603 --> 00:05:31.914
That's Module 1.

93
00:05:31.914 --> 00:05:36.530
Next, the Microsoft cloud baseline
and the tools that measure it.

94
00:05:40.280 --> 00:05:42.068
Module 2 gets concrete.

95
00:05:42.068 --> 00:05:46.505
CISA's cloud directive names exact
settings and tells you how to prove them.

96
00:05:46.505 --> 00:05:51.084
We'll cover what it requires, the
two assessment tools agencies run,

97
00:05:51.084 --> 00:05:55.430
and everything those tools
can't see beyond Microsoft 365.

98
00:05:59.180 --> 00:06:02.480
CISA's cloud directive
has six required actions.

99
00:06:02.480 --> 00:06:05.506
Half of them are paperwork:
list your tenants,

100
00:06:05.506 --> 00:06:08.368
deploy the tools, explain your deviations.

101
00:06:08.368 --> 00:06:10.056
Agencies did those quickly.

102
00:06:10.056 --> 00:06:14.280
The other half is the real point:
implement the mandatory settings,

103
00:06:14.280 --> 00:06:19.043
keep up as they change, and baseline
every new tenant before it's authorized.

104
00:06:19.043 --> 00:06:21.158
In practice that means remediation,

105
00:06:21.158 --> 00:06:24.543
and much of it depends on
architecture outside the tenant.

106
00:06:24.543 --> 00:06:27.043
Scope is tighter than people think, too.

107
00:06:27.043 --> 00:06:29.867
It's production
software-as-a-service tenants only,

108
00:06:29.867 --> 00:06:32.556
so cloud infrastructure
subscriptions are out.

109
00:06:32.556 --> 00:06:36.180
So measure compliance by what's
fixed, not by what's reported.

110
00:06:36.180 --> 00:06:39.390
Next, what the mandatory list contains.

111
00:06:43.140 --> 00:06:47.090
The mandatory list is a subset
of CISA's full baselines.

112
00:06:47.090 --> 00:06:49.871
For Microsoft 365 it's fifty policies,

113
00:06:49.871 --> 00:06:53.728
out of well over a hundred
in the baseline documents.

114
00:06:53.728 --> 00:06:57.588
Treating every recommendation
as binding is one mistake;

115
00:06:57.588 --> 00:07:01.127
treating the list as all of
good practice is the other.

116
00:07:01.127 --> 00:07:02.715
The list also moves.

117
00:07:02.715 --> 00:07:06.096
Policies get revised, the
next tool release adds more,

118
00:07:06.096 --> 00:07:10.015
and Google Workspace is now in
scope with a December deadline.

119
00:07:10.015 --> 00:07:13.356
The rule is simple: CISA's
page governs, so track it,

120
00:07:13.356 --> 00:07:16.615
and pin the baseline
version you assess against.

121
00:07:16.615 --> 00:07:21.290
Next, how results reach CISA,
and where the directive stops.

122
00:07:25.040 --> 00:07:27.376
There are four ways to report to CISA,

123
00:07:27.376 --> 00:07:30.315
from fully hosted to
manual quarterly uploads.

124
00:07:30.315 --> 00:07:34.343
Whichever you pick, the evidence
is the tool's own results file,

125
00:07:34.343 --> 00:07:39.240
never renamed, and any approved deviation
shows up as omitted, with a reason.

126
00:07:39.240 --> 00:07:42.903
That file and that rationale matter
more than the pretty report.

127
00:07:42.903 --> 00:07:44.290
And remember the edge.

128
00:07:44.290 --> 00:07:46.915
The directive covers
cloud tenant settings.

129
00:07:46.915 --> 00:07:49.997
It says nothing about the
on-premises directory,

130
00:07:49.997 --> 00:07:53.465
old authentication protocols,
endpoints or networks.

131
00:07:53.465 --> 00:07:56.978
A perfect tenant can sit
on top of a messy forest.

132
00:07:56.978 --> 00:08:00.950
Next, the two tools agencies
use to measure all this.

133
00:08:04.700 --> 00:08:08.850
Agencies often run two free tools
and then wonder if they need both.

134
00:08:08.850 --> 00:08:11.513
They do, because they
ask different questions.

135
00:08:11.513 --> 00:08:17.400
CISA's tool asks whether your Microsoft
365 tenant meets the federal baseline.

136
00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:20.530
Microsoft's tool asks how
mature your environment

137
00:08:20.530 --> 00:08:23.212
is against Microsoft's
Zero Trust guidance.

138
00:08:23.212 --> 00:08:27.824
They run differently, label findings
differently and score severity differently.

139
00:08:27.824 --> 00:08:30.137
And read-only doesn't mean low privilege.

140
00:08:30.137 --> 00:08:35.388
Both need broad read roles and an admin
consent, and some products need admin roles.

141
00:08:35.388 --> 00:08:39.350
Test the exact roles in your tenant
before you write the run book.

142
00:08:39.350 --> 00:08:43.130
Next, where the two tools
overlap and where they don't.

143
00:08:46.880 --> 00:08:51.905
The tools overlap mostly on identity, and
now partly on email threat protection.

144
00:08:51.905 --> 00:08:55.642
But overlap means the same
area, not the same checks.

145
00:08:55.642 --> 00:08:58.821
Microsoft's tool goes
much deeper on identity,

146
00:08:58.821 --> 00:09:02.167
and it alone covers devices,
networking and data.

147
00:09:02.167 --> 00:09:06.875
CISA's tool alone covers mail flow,
Teams, sharing and Power Platform,

148
00:09:06.875 --> 00:09:10.343
and almost half the mandatory
policies live there.

149
00:09:10.343 --> 00:09:12.873
So dropping either one leaves real gaps,

150
00:09:12.873 --> 00:09:17.629
and dropping CISA's tool also stops
the reporting the directive requires.

151
00:09:17.629 --> 00:09:20.418
Keep both, and reconcile them by control.

152
00:09:20.418 --> 00:09:24.570
Next, everything outside Microsoft 365.

153
00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:30.193
Outside Microsoft 365,

154
00:09:30.193 --> 00:09:35.220
every provider's tool tells you
its own limits, in its own words.

155
00:09:35.220 --> 00:09:37.920
One covers only a subset of controls.

156
00:09:37.920 --> 00:09:40.320
Another calls its compliance view partial.

157
00:09:40.320 --> 00:09:44.720
The hypervisor guidance warns its
assumptions may not fit your environment.

158
00:09:44.720 --> 00:09:47.999
And a FedRAMP certification
covers the provider's service,

159
00:09:47.999 --> 00:09:51.345
not how you configured it or
connected it to everything else.

160
00:09:51.345 --> 00:09:56.145
So running every vendor's tool gives
you a pile of reports, one per vendor.

161
00:09:56.145 --> 00:10:00.345
The agency is the only party that
can hold one posture across them.

162
00:10:00.345 --> 00:10:04.670
Next, what has to be reconciled
before any score means something.

163
00:10:08.420 --> 00:10:11.495
Before you assess, reconcile three things.

164
00:10:11.495 --> 00:10:14.383
One inventory, so
nothing is counted twice.

165
00:10:14.383 --> 00:10:18.370
One naming authority, so a workload
has the same name everywhere.

166
00:10:18.370 --> 00:10:22.365
And no implicit trust from any
boundary, whether that's a VPN,

167
00:10:22.365 --> 00:10:25.708
a default cloud network or
a flat virtual segment.

168
00:10:25.708 --> 00:10:27.219
Then harden two things.

169
00:10:27.219 --> 00:10:31.149
The hypervisor management plane
gets assessed as its own surface,

170
00:10:31.149 --> 00:10:33.007
because guest tools can't see it.

171
00:10:33.007 --> 00:10:37.974
And the unified plane you just built becomes
the most valuable target in the estate,

172
00:10:37.974 --> 00:10:41.357
so it gets top-tier admin
controls and its own monitoring.

173
00:10:41.357 --> 00:10:42.757
That closes Part 1.

174
00:10:42.757 --> 00:10:45.200
Part 2 is about who owns it.

175
00:10:48.950 --> 00:10:51.925
Module 3 is about ownership and order.

176
00:10:51.925 --> 00:10:55.975
Every pillar has an owner, but the
overall posture usually doesn't.

177
00:10:55.975 --> 00:10:58.761
We'll name who decides what,
then look at why you close

178
00:10:58.761 --> 00:11:02.350
the known findings before
running the maturity assessment.

179
00:11:06.100 --> 00:11:08.125
Programs rarely lack owners.

180
00:11:08.125 --> 00:11:10.547
They lack an owner for the posture itself,

181
00:11:10.547 --> 00:11:13.825
the person who can say what
the agency-wide score covers.

182
00:11:13.825 --> 00:11:15.382
That can't be a pillar owner,

183
00:11:15.382 --> 00:11:19.487
and it can't be whoever manages the
biggest cloud vendor relationship.

184
00:11:19.487 --> 00:11:24.138
In the program's model, a Zero Trust
program lead owns the score's definition,

185
00:11:24.138 --> 00:11:27.288
cross-pillar routing and
the assessment calendar.

186
00:11:27.288 --> 00:11:30.338
Risk acceptance stays with
the authorizing official.

187
00:11:30.338 --> 00:11:31.850
And here's the subtle part.

188
00:11:31.850 --> 00:11:35.797
Omitting a policy from a tool's
report looks like configuration,

189
00:11:35.797 --> 00:11:37.212
but it's a risk decision.

190
00:11:37.212 --> 00:11:40.626
The engineer types it; someone
with authority decides it.

191
00:11:40.626 --> 00:11:43.600
Next, the matrix that pins that down.

192
00:11:47.350 --> 00:11:51.875
The responsibility matrix gives
every decision exactly one decider.

193
00:11:51.875 --> 00:11:53.625
A few rows carry the weight.

194
00:11:53.625 --> 00:11:57.108
The program lead defines what
the whole-agency score covers,

195
00:11:57.108 --> 00:11:59.825
because everyone else has
a reason to shrink it.

196
00:11:59.825 --> 00:12:03.050
An exception with an end
date is the CISO's call.

197
00:12:03.050 --> 00:12:06.160
One with no end date is a
standing risk acceptance,

198
00:12:06.160 --> 00:12:08.587
so it goes to the authorizing official.

199
00:12:08.587 --> 00:12:11.208
Drift goes to the
authorizing official too,

200
00:12:11.208 --> 00:12:13.900
because it's a change to
an authorized system.

201
00:12:13.900 --> 00:12:18.900
And a vendor's own tool is evidence for
its own platform, never the agency's score.

202
00:12:18.900 --> 00:12:22.140
Next, the roles people
need in each system.

203
00:12:25.890 --> 00:12:29.265
Decision rights don't change
what a console lets someone do.

204
00:12:29.265 --> 00:12:33.665
Roles do, and these tools need
broad read access to every tenant.

205
00:12:33.665 --> 00:12:35.679
So the operators run as readers,

206
00:12:35.679 --> 00:12:39.165
with admin roles only where
a product truly needs them.

207
00:12:39.165 --> 00:12:41.358
The one-time consent is granted by an

208
00:12:41.358 --> 00:12:44.428
administrator who doesn't
then run the assessment.

209
00:12:44.428 --> 00:12:48.862
Those assessment accounts are among
the most sensitive identities you have,

210
00:12:48.862 --> 00:12:53.227
so make them eligible rather than standing,
and send their sign-ins to the SOC.

211
00:12:53.227 --> 00:12:56.458
Even the tool's telemetry
setting is a CISO decision,

212
00:12:56.458 --> 00:12:58.440
not something decided on the day.

213
00:12:58.440 --> 00:13:00.750
Next, the order of work.

214
00:13:04.500 --> 00:13:06.300
Here's the discipline of this book.

215
00:13:06.300 --> 00:13:08.450
The assessment tools are thermometers.

216
00:13:08.450 --> 00:13:11.062
They measure deviation;
they don't fix any.

217
00:13:11.062 --> 00:13:14.273
Run them as your program before
the architecture exists,

218
00:13:14.273 --> 00:13:16.937
and you get an accurate
list that never shrinks.

219
00:13:16.937 --> 00:13:19.938
So the program's sequence
puts them at step five.

220
00:13:19.938 --> 00:13:24.326
First understand the gaps, design
the fix, build it and govern it.

221
00:13:24.326 --> 00:13:28.850
Then the tools confirm the built state,
and the authorizing official decides.

222
00:13:28.850 --> 00:13:31.687
That's the same order as
NIST's risk framework.

223
00:13:31.687 --> 00:13:33.570
And it means building naming,

224
00:13:33.570 --> 00:13:37.413
device identity and the retirement
of old protocols first.

225
00:13:37.413 --> 00:13:40.290
Next, what to do with each finding today.

226
00:13:44.040 --> 00:13:46.690
Not every finding has to
wait for architecture.

227
00:13:46.690 --> 00:13:49.402
Each one goes to exactly one response.

228
00:13:49.402 --> 00:13:52.764
If it's a simple setting with
no dependencies, change it now.

229
00:13:52.764 --> 00:13:55.363
If it depends on a layer
that doesn't exist yet,

230
00:13:55.363 --> 00:13:57.890
it gets an owner and a
date against that work.

231
00:13:57.890 --> 00:13:59.964
If the deviation is needed for now,

232
00:13:59.964 --> 00:14:03.890
it becomes a dated risk acceptance
by the authorizing official.

233
00:14:03.890 --> 00:14:07.465
The only real failure is a
finding with no response at all.

234
00:14:07.465 --> 00:14:12.490
Then a continuous layer tracks every
open item as it ages, run to run.

235
00:14:12.490 --> 00:14:14.353
That closes Module 3.

236
00:14:14.353 --> 00:14:16.640
Next, running the assessment.

237
00:14:20.390 --> 00:14:24.365
Module 4 is the run itself,
and what happens to the output.

238
00:14:24.365 --> 00:14:27.077
A saved report is an opinion from one day.

239
00:14:27.077 --> 00:14:30.148
The same output, sealed
with its provenance so

240
00:14:30.148 --> 00:14:33.139
an assessor can rebuild
it later, is evidence.

241
00:14:33.139 --> 00:14:34.460
Let's see how.

242
00:14:38.210 --> 00:14:41.672
Five decisions come before
either tool touches production.

243
00:14:41.672 --> 00:14:46.273
Scope: which tenants and subscriptions
this run covers, written down.

244
00:14:46.273 --> 00:14:50.114
The host: a dedicated Windows
machine inside your boundary,

245
00:14:50.114 --> 00:14:54.111
a clean one for each tool, never
a shared internet build runner.

246
00:14:54.111 --> 00:14:59.272
Accounts: a dedicated reader for each
tool, with consent granted separately.

247
00:14:59.272 --> 00:15:04.115
Output: treat every report as sensitive,
because it's a map of your weaknesses,

248
00:15:04.115 --> 00:15:08.573
so move it unaltered into the evidence
store and delete the local copy.

249
00:15:08.573 --> 00:15:11.491
And logging: the assessment
accounts' sign-ins

250
00:15:11.491 --> 00:15:13.948
belong in your agency logging plan now.

251
00:15:13.948 --> 00:15:16.090
Next, the run itself.

252
00:15:19.840 --> 00:15:22.278
For both tools, three habits matter.

253
00:15:22.278 --> 00:15:24.849
Pin the version, and
record it with every run,

254
00:15:24.849 --> 00:15:28.564
along with the configuration
file that holds your exceptions.

255
00:15:28.564 --> 00:15:31.527
Keep the results file
exactly as the tool wrote it.

256
00:15:31.527 --> 00:15:35.303
Never edit or rename it;
only hash it and copy it.

257
00:15:35.303 --> 00:15:36.715
And expect quirks.

258
00:15:36.715 --> 00:15:39.827
Microsoft's tool is a
fast-moving community project,

259
00:15:39.827 --> 00:15:44.315
it can run for more than a day on a big
tenant, and you shouldn't stop it halfway.

260
00:15:44.315 --> 00:15:47.681
One honest gap, too: the
Google Workspace assessor

261
00:15:47.681 --> 00:15:50.965
isn't wired into the
program's evidence chain yet.

262
00:15:50.965 --> 00:15:54.150
Next, how a result becomes evidence.

263
00:15:57.900 --> 00:16:01.088
Each tool becomes an input
to one evidence chain.

264
00:16:01.088 --> 00:16:05.446
Every result, passes included, gets
wrapped with where it came from,

265
00:16:05.446 --> 00:16:08.062
who issued it and a
hash of the raw output.

266
00:16:08.062 --> 00:16:12.108
The results from one run are sealed
into a bundle that rebuilds byte

267
00:16:12.108 --> 00:16:16.225
for byte from the same inputs, so an
assessor can check it months later.

268
00:16:16.225 --> 00:16:19.738
Then it's emitted in NIST's
machine-readable assessment format.

269
00:16:19.738 --> 00:16:23.986
Follow one failed legacy sign-in check
through those four hops, and you can

270
00:16:23.986 --> 00:16:28.374
walk back from the final record to the
raw result without re-running the tool.

271
00:16:28.374 --> 00:16:31.920
Next, how to read the
results before counting them.

272
00:16:35.670 --> 00:16:37.907
Before you count results, read them.

273
00:16:37.907 --> 00:16:42.408
Only fails, warnings and items flagged
for investigation are findings.

274
00:16:42.408 --> 00:16:45.407
A skipped check isn't a
pass, and it isn't a gap.

275
00:16:45.407 --> 00:16:50.170
It's absence of evidence, often because
a license or a connection was missing.

276
00:16:50.170 --> 00:16:54.334
In a government cloud, some premium
features simply aren't available,

277
00:16:54.334 --> 00:16:57.695
so a skipped or failed premium
check may not be a real gap.

278
00:16:57.695 --> 00:17:00.689
Score it as failed and
you overstate the problem;

279
00:17:00.689 --> 00:17:03.757
score it as passed and you
overstate your posture.

280
00:17:03.757 --> 00:17:06.407
Record it as a boundary finding instead.

281
00:17:06.407 --> 00:17:09.300
Next, scoring and reconciling.

282
00:17:13.050 --> 00:17:15.978
Findings get ranked by
severity and effort,

283
00:17:15.978 --> 00:17:19.475
so a serious problem with a
cheap fix rises to the top.

284
00:17:19.475 --> 00:17:22.097
Quick wins go into the next change window;

285
00:17:22.097 --> 00:17:25.337
big projects go onto a
pillar roadmap with funding.

286
00:17:25.337 --> 00:17:30.134
That scoring is the program's own method,
documented so anyone can check it,

287
00:17:30.134 --> 00:17:33.200
and a top priority still
isn't a risk acceptance.

288
00:17:33.200 --> 00:17:37.187
Then reconcile the two tools
by control, not tool to tool.

289
00:17:37.187 --> 00:17:39.199
When both flag the same setting,

290
00:17:39.199 --> 00:17:42.988
that's one finding with two
pieces of evidence and one owner.

291
00:17:42.988 --> 00:17:45.920
Next, exceptions and re-assessment.

292
00:17:49.670 --> 00:17:53.657
Some findings won't be fixed soon,
and that's fine if it's governed.

293
00:17:53.657 --> 00:17:56.682
Exceptions live in the
tool's configuration file,

294
00:17:56.682 --> 00:18:00.519
under version control, with an
expiry date and a named approver.

295
00:18:00.519 --> 00:18:04.910
Whatever isn't fixed or excepted goes
on the plan of action and milestones,

296
00:18:04.910 --> 00:18:08.220
with an owner and the evidence
that will prove it closed.

297
00:18:08.220 --> 00:18:12.194
Then re-run on a calendar and
after each remediation wave,

298
00:18:12.194 --> 00:18:15.745
so findings close by evidence,
not by ticket status.

299
00:18:15.745 --> 00:18:17.386
Each run adds an interval,

300
00:18:17.386 --> 00:18:21.607
and anything that newly breaks
goes to the authorizing official.

301
00:18:21.607 --> 00:18:24.530
Next, how this maps to controls.

302
00:18:28.280 --> 00:18:31.443
The requirements map is
careful about what it claims.

303
00:18:31.443 --> 00:18:35.219
It helps you reuse the design
and evidence in your own plans;

304
00:18:35.219 --> 00:18:36.992
it doesn't certify anything.

305
00:18:36.992 --> 00:18:40.299
It shows which controls Zero
Trust evidence supports,

306
00:18:40.299 --> 00:18:44.930
which book builds each capability, and
what an assessor should expect to see.

307
00:18:44.930 --> 00:18:49.280
It's honest about gaps, too, like
incident response and data protection,

308
00:18:49.280 --> 00:18:51.155
which aren't designed here yet.

309
00:18:51.155 --> 00:18:56.705
And one detail worth knowing: no control
in the NIST catalog mentions Zero Trust.

310
00:18:56.705 --> 00:18:59.417
The evidence supports
controls you already have.

311
00:18:59.417 --> 00:19:01.580
Let's finish with the decisions.

312
00:19:05.330 --> 00:19:07.105
None of this needs a procurement.

313
00:19:07.105 --> 00:19:12.005
This quarter, find out which pillar evidence
can be re-run without the operator.

314
00:19:12.005 --> 00:19:16.093
Name an owner for the whole posture,
separate from any cloud vendor.

315
00:19:16.093 --> 00:19:18.642
Bring one tool into the evidence chain.

316
00:19:18.642 --> 00:19:22.543
Move exceptions into version
control, with expiry dates.

317
00:19:22.543 --> 00:19:25.067
Send drift to the authorizing official.

318
00:19:25.067 --> 00:19:28.616
Model the cloud boundary,
reconcile the inventory first,

319
00:19:28.616 --> 00:19:32.843
and time-box privileged access,
including the assessment accounts.

320
00:19:32.843 --> 00:19:35.654
No tool certifies Zero Trust maturity.

321
00:19:35.654 --> 00:19:39.242
The evidence supports the decisions,
and named people make them.

322
00:19:39.242 --> 00:19:40.060
Thank you.
